.\" tvmail-backend(1) man page.
.TH TVMAIL\-BACKEND 1 "November 2026" "tvmail 1.4.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tvmail\-backend \- the part of tvmail that does the actual work
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tvmail\-backend
.I subcommand
.RI [ args ]
.br
.B tvmail\-backend
.RB [ \-\-debug [ FILE ] ]
.RB [ \-\-trace [ FILE ] ]
.RB [ \-\-help | \-\-version ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tvmail\-backend
is a small, standard\-library\-only Python program that performs every
mail\-format operation on behalf of
.BR tvmail (1):
listing mailboxes, decoding MIME, composing message skeletons, handing outgoing
mail to
.BR sendmail (8),
deleting, marking, saving drafts, and expanding
.I ~/.mailrc
aliases.
.PP
.BR tvmail (1)
runs it as a child process and reads its output.
It is documented \(em and packaged \(em separately because it is perfectly
usable on its own from a shell or a script; the Turbo Vision front end holds no
mail logic that this does not.
.PP
It runs in one of two
.BR modes .
On the host that owns the mail store it reads mbox files and hands outgoing
mail to
.BR sendmail (8)
(\fBlocal\fP mode).
On any other machine it is an IMAP/SMTP client of that host (\fBremote\fP
mode): the same subcommands, but backed by
.BR imaplib / smtplib .
See
.B MODES
below.
.PP
Each subcommand prints its result to standard output and exits
.B 0
on success,
.B 1
on a usage or not\-found error, or
.B 2
on a runtime failure.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI "--debug " [FILE]
Write diagnostic logging to
.IR FILE .
If omitted, use
.IR debug.tvmail.backend.log .
.TP
.BI "--trace " [FILE]
Profile the backend with Python's standard
.BR cProfile (1)
and write its statistics to
.IR FILE .
If omitted, use
.IR trace.tvmail.backend.log .
.TP
.BI "--service " NAME
Which service the verb is about; before the verb.
Default
.BR mail ;
the others are listed by
.BR services .
An unknown name is an error.
.TP
.B --help
Show command-line help and exit.
.TP
.B --version
Print the backend version and exit.
.SH "MAILBOX ARGUMENT"
Wherever a subcommand takes a
.I MBOX
argument it accepts either a filesystem path or one of these shorthands:
.TP
.B spool
.I $MAIL
or
.IR /var/mail/$USER .
.TP
.B mbox
.IR ~/mbox .
.TP
.B drafts
.IR $folder/drafts
(default
.IR ~/Mail/drafts ),
where
.I $folder
is
.B "set folder"
from the mailrc files.
.TP
.B sent
.IR $folder/sent ,
or the mailrc
.B "set record"
value.
.B send
files a copy here.
.TP
.B spam
.IR $folder/Junk
(remote:
.BR Junk ).
Where
.B pull
files server\-tagged spam.
.TP
.B trash
.IR ~/.local/share/tvmail/trash.mbox .
.TP
.B dead
.I $DEAD
or
.IR ~/dead.letter ,
treated as a single\-message mailbox.
.PP
In
.B remote
mode the shorthands map to IMAP folders instead \(em
.BR spool " \(->" " INBOX" ,
.BR mbox " \(->" " Archive" ,
.BR trash " \(->" " Trash" ,
.BR drafts " \(->" " Drafts" ,
.BR sent " \(->" " Sent" ,
.BR spam " \(->" " Junk"
\(em overridable in
.IR tvmail.conf .
.B dead
is always the local file.
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
.BI "list " [MBOX]
One line per message:
.IR "index" ,
tab,
.I flags
.RB ( N
new or
.B .
seen), tab,
.IR date ,
tab,
.IR from ,
tab,
.IR subject .
A mailbox that does not exist yet lists as empty.
.TP
.BI "counts " "\fR[\fPMBOX ...\fR]\fP"
One line per mailbox:
.IR name ,
.IR total ,
.I unseen
(TAB-separated).
Default is every folder except
.BR dead .
Over IMAP this is a STATUS request, so it does not disturb the open folder;
a folder that does not exist counts as 0 0.
.TP
.B services
One line per service in the Services pane:
.IR name ,
.IR kind ,
.I label
(TAB-separated), in the order of
.B [services] order
in
.IR tvmail.conf .
The services are
.BR mail ", " lists " and " news
(Usenet); one whose configuration section is not filled in
.RB ( "[lists] address" " and remote mode, " "[news] host" )
is left out, and with none left it is
.BR mail .
.TP
.B folders
One line per folder of the service (\fB--service\fP, default mail):
.IR label ,
.IR MBOX ,
.IR total ,
.I unseen
(TAB-separated).
.I MBOX
is what the other verbs take.
The counts are those of
.BR counts .
.TP
.BI "search " "MBOX QUERY"
[\fB--limit\fP N] [\fB--budget\fP SECONDS]
The list numbers (as \fBlist\fP prints them), one per line, of the messages with every word of
.I QUERY
(quotes keep a phrase together) in From, To, Subject or the text, any case.  IMAP: the server\(aqs SEARCH;
a local mailbox: read here;
Usenet (\fB--service news\fP): the headers from the overview, the texts fetched for the newest
\fB--limit\fP articles (default \fB[news] window\fP) for at most \fB--budget\fP seconds (default 30), after which a last line
\(lq# searched the newest N of M articles\(rq says how far it got.
.TP
.B lists-available
Ask the list server (\fB[lists] address\fP) which lists there are: mails it
.BR LIST ,
waits for the answer mail (\fB--wait\fP seconds, default \fB[lists] wait\fP) and prints it: an
.B OK
or
.B ERROR
line, then
.I name
and description lines.
.TP
.BI "lists-subscribe " NAME " [--realname " "First Last" ]
.BR SUBSCRIBE .
When the answer is OK the folder \fBLists/\fP\fINAME\fP is made at once.
.TP
.BI "lists-signoff " NAME
.BR SIGNOFF .
The folder and its mail stay.
.TP
.BI "lists-digest " "NAME " "on" | off
.B SET
.I NAME
.BR DIGEST " or " NODIGEST .
.PP
With
.B --service lists
the verbs
.BR list ", " show ", " raw ", " mark ", " counts ", " search ", " delete ", " parts ", " save ", " folders " and " compose-template
are the Mail ones on the IMAP folders under \fB[lists] prefix\fP (the \fIMBOX\fP is e.g. \fBLists/alpha\fP); a reply and a new
message are addressed to the list (its \fBList-Post\fP, else \fINAME\fP@\fB[lists] domain\fP).  Lists needs remote mode.
.TP
.BR "news-groups " [PATTERN]
Every group on the news server (\fB[news] host\fP) matching the wildmat
.IR PATTERN :
.IR name ,
.IR first ,
.IR last ,
.IR status ,
.I subscribed
(\fB*\fP), description (TAB-separated).
.TP
.BI "news-subscribe " GROUP
Subscribe to a group (the server must have it).
.BR news-unsubscribe " and " news-catchup
(mark every article read) take a group the same way.
.PP
With
.B --service news
the verbs
.BR list ", " show ", " raw ", " mark ", " counts ", " folders ", " compose-template " and " send
are about Usenet: the MBOX argument is a group name and an index is an article number.
.B list
takes
.BI "--limit " N
(the newest N article numbers; default
.B [news] window
of 500) and prints, after the subject, the Message-ID and References.
What you have read is kept in
.IR ~/.local/share/tvmail/newsrc-HOST .
.B send
posts the article on standard input (it needs a Newsgroups header); delete, purge and
empty-trash are mail only.
.TP
.BI "show " "IDX " [MBOX]
Decoded headers followed by the best available text of message
.IR IDX .
.TP
.BI "raw " "IDX " [MBOX]
The message exactly as stored.
.TP
.BI "parts " "IDX " [MBOX]
One line per MIME part:
.IR "part\-number" ,
.IR content\-type ,
.IR filename ,
.IR bytes .
.TP
.BI "save " "IDX PARTNO DEST " [MBOX]
Write the decoded payload of one MIME part to
.IR DEST .
.TP
.BI "delete " "IDX " "\fR[\fPIDX ...\fR]\fP " "\fR[\fP--mbox " "MBOX\fR]\fP " "\fR[\fP--trash " "DEST\fR]\fP"
Remove one or more messages.
With
.BR \-\-trash ,
each is appended to
.I DEST
first.
A
.I .bak
of the mailbox is kept.
.TP
.BI "purge " "\fR[\fPMBOX\fR]\fP " "\fR[\fPfilters\fR]\fP " "\fR[\fP--to-folder " "DEST" " | --expunge\fR]\fP " "\fR[\fP-n\fR]\fP"
Move every message in
.I MBOX
that matches
.B all
of the given filters \(em substring (case\-insensitive) on
.BR \-\-from ", " \-\-subject ", " \-\-to ,
an
.B \-\-older\-than
age in whole days, and/or
.BR \-\-seen / \-\-unseen
\(em into
.I DEST
(a mailbox shorthand or path; default
.BR trash ).
.B \-\-expunge
deletes them instead.
At least one filter is required; with none it refuses to run.
.B \-n
(\c
.BR \-\-dry\-run )
lists what would move and changes nothing.
Handy from
.BR cron
on the master:
.RS
.EX
tvmail\-backend purge --from "Cron Daemon" --older-than 14
tvmail\-backend purge --subject "***SPAM***" --to-folder spam
.EE
.RE
.TP
.BI "vacation " "status" | on | off | addresses " \fR[\fP--message-file " "FILE" "\fR]\fP \fR[\fP--subject " "S" "\fR]\fP \fR[\fP--days " "N" "\fR]\fP \fR[\fP--start " "D" "\fR]\fP \fR[\fP--end " "D" "\fR]\fP \fR[\fP--addresses " "A,B" "\fR]\fP \fR[\fP--force\fR]\fP"
Out\-of\-office auto\-reply.
An automatic reply must be sent by the machine that receives the mail, so this
manages one Sieve
.B vacation
script (default name
.IR tvmail\-vacation )
on the master over ManageSieve (RFC 5804, port 4190; see
.B [sieve]
under
.BR MODES ),
logged in as the invoking user.
.B status
prints
.IR "state: none|off|on" ,
.I other_active:
(another Sieve script is active),
.IR subject ,
.IR days ,
.IR start ,
.IR end ,
.IR addresses ,
a blank line and the message.
.B on
installs and activates the script (the message comes from
.BR \-\-message\-file );
.B off
deactivates it and keeps it for next time.
Dates are
.IR YYYY\-MM\-DD ,
inclusive; the same sender is answered at most once every
.B \-\-days
(default 7).
Only one Sieve script can be active, so
.B on
refuses to replace another one unless given
.BR \-\-force .
.IP
The server answers only mail addressed to one of
.BR \-\-addresses .
A master such as cpbee hides behind one public mailbox: it fetches that
mailbox over POP and delivers to a local user, and rewrites local senders back
to the public address on the way out, much like a NAT.
Mail therefore arrives addressed to the public address.
By default the list is built from
.BR "[sieve] addresses" ,
.BR "[smtp] from" ,
the user of
.IR ~/.mail 's
.B mailer
URL, on the master the user's own lines in
.IR /etc/postfix/generic
(never the shared
.B @host
lines), and the local identity
.IR user@host .
.B addresses
prints that table with where each entry came from.
Everything is per user.
Enable the server side with
.IR configure/enable\-managesieve.sh .
.TP
.BR "empty\-trash " "\fR[\fP-n\fR]\fP"
Permanently delete every message in the
.B trash
folder.
Equivalent to
.BR "purge trash \-\-all \-\-expunge" ;
.B \-\-all
is accepted only for
.BR trash .
.B \-n
only counts.
In local mode a
.I .bak
of the trash mailbox is kept; over IMAP there is no undo.
.TP
.BI "mark " "IDX " "read|unread " [MBOX]
Set or clear the Status flag
.RB ( \(rs Seen
over IMAP).
.TP
.BI "compose\-template " "\fR[\fP--to " "A\fR]\fP " "\fR[\fP--subject " "S\fR]\fP " "\fR[\fP--in-reply-to " "IDX " "\fR[\fP--reply-all\fR]\fP\fR]\fP " "\fR[\fP--forward " "IDX\fR]\fP " [MBOX]
Print a draft skeleton: a
.BR From: ,
.BR To: ,
.B Subject:
block, a blank line, and (for a reply) the quoted original.
.B --reply-all
(with
.BR --in-reply-to )
also fills in a
.B Cc:
of the original message's To/Cc, minus whatever landed in the new
.BR To: .
.B --forward
prefixes the subject
.RB ( Fwd: ,
not doubled if already present) and quotes the original message, headers
included, with
.B To:
left blank.
.TP
.BI "send " "\fR[\fP--from " "A\fR]\fP " "\fR[\fP--to " "A ...\fR]\fP " "\fR[\fP--subject " "S\fR]\fP"
Read an RFC\ 822 message (or a bare body, with
.BR \-\-to )
on standard input, submit it (\c
.BR sendmail (8)
locally, SMTP in remote mode) and file a copy in
.BR sent .
.B alias
and
.B group
names in
.BR To: ,
.B Cc:
and
.B Bcc:
are expanded from the mailrc files before delivery.
A blank
.B "[folders] sent"
in
.I tvmail.conf
disables the copy.
.TP
.B save\-draft
Read an RFC\ 822 message on standard input and append it to the
.B drafts
mailbox, adding a
.B Date:
header if absent.
.TP
.B aliases
Print the address book:
.I name
tab
.IR "expanded, addresses" ,
one per line.
.TP
.B pull
In
.B local
mode: run the puller \(em
.B $TVMAIL_PULL
or
.B [local] pull
in
.IR tvmail.conf ,
else
.B mail\-pull
(mail\-setup's wrapper around
.B pop\-pull
or
.BR getmail ),
else
.B pop\-pull
\(em then sweep the inbox for spam.
In
.B remote
mode: just the spam sweep \(em the master does the fetching.
The sweep moves inbox messages whose
.B Subject:
contains the
.B "[spam] subject"
tag (default
.BR ***SPAM*** ,
what SpamAssassin prepends) into the
.B spam
folder.
.B "[folders] spam"
blank turns it off.
.TP
.B ping
Print
.BR pong .
A liveness check.
.TP
.B serve
Persistent mode.
Read one request (a normal argv) per line on standard input and write a framed
reply per request:
.RB ` "<status> <nbytes>" '
and a newline, then
.I nbytes
bytes.
The mbox / MIME machinery \(em and, in remote mode, the IMAP connection \(em
stay warm, so
.BR tvmail (1)
pays start\-up once instead of once per keystroke.
.B send ", " save\-draft ", " pull
and
.B serve
itself are refused inside the loop.
.SH MODES
The mode is chosen by
.I ~/.config/tvmail/tvmail.conf
(an INI file;
.B $TVMAIL_CONF
overrides the path):
.RS
.EX
[service]
mode   = local | remote

[imap]   host= port=993 ssl=true verify=true cafile= user=
[smtp]   host= port=587 starttls=true auth=true verify=true cafile= from= user=
[folders] saved=Archive  trash=Trash  drafts=Drafts
[sieve]  host= port=4190 user= tls=starttls verify= cafile= script=tvmail-vacation addresses= plus=false tag=
.EE
.RE
.PP
.B mode
must be
.B local
or
.BR remote .
Unset (or no
.I tvmail.conf
at all) defaults to
.BR local ,
so the master needs no configuration file.
A client must set
.B mode = remote
explicitly.
.B $TVMAIL_MODE
overrides it for one run.
.PP
.B host
/
.B port
/
.B user
left unset in
.I tvmail.conf
fall back to GNU Mailutils'
.I ~/.mail
.RB ( $TVMAIL_MU_CONF
overrides the path):
.RS
.EX
mailbox { mailbox\-pattern "imap://chris@cpbee:143/INBOX"; };
mailer  { url "smtp://cpbee:25"; };
.EE
.RE
.PP
Passwords are never kept in
.IR tvmail.conf .
They are read from
.I ~/.mu-tickets
.RB ( $TVMAIL_MU_TICKETS
overrides the path) \(em lines of
.RI scheme://user:pass@host
(scheme
.B *
matches any), URL\-encoded, mode 0600 \(em then
.I ~/.netrc
.RI ( "machine <host> login <user> password <pw>" ,
mode 0600), or
.B $TVMAIL_IMAP_PASS
/
.BR $TVMAIL_SMTP_PASS .
With
.B "[smtp] auth = false"
(a relay that trusts the LAN) no password is needed;
.B "[smtp] from"
forces the sender identity so the master relays without rewriting.
.B "[imap] verify = false"
and
.B "[smtp] verify = false"
(each with an optional
.BR cafile )
skip certificate\-chain checking \(em needed for a LAN server's stock
self\-signed cert, the same one
.BR mail (1)
does not check either.
A LAN server with a certificate from your own CA needs no such thing: leave
.B verify
on and set
.B cafile
(a leading
.B ~
is your home) to the file holding that CA's certificates.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/tvmail/tvmail.conf
Selects local vs. remote and holds the IMAP/SMTP host settings.
See
.BR MODES .
A commented
.I tvmail.conf.example
ships with the source.
.TP
.I ~/.mail
GNU Mailutils' own config \(em its
.B mailbox
.RB { mailbox-pattern }
and
.B mailer
.RB { url }
blocks are a fallback for any of
.IR tvmail.conf 's
.B [imap]
/
.B [smtp]
.BR host / port / user
left unset.
.TP
.B password_cmd
An option in
.IR tvmail.conf ,
off by default: under
.B [auth]
(or
.BR [imap] ", " [smtp] ", " [sieve] ", " [news]
for one service; or
.B $TVMAIL_PASSWORD_CMD
for one run), a command such as
.B auth get {scheme} {host} {user}
that prints the password (first line) and exits non\-zero when it has none. \" allowlist secret
The three words are filled in per service
.RB ( imaps ", " smtp ", " sieve ", " nntp );
an empty
.B {user}
leaves its word out.
No shell runs it, its error output is dropped and it is given 15 seconds.
Asked after the
.B $TVMAIL_*_PASS
variable and before
.IR ~/.mu-tickets ;
a command that finds nothing falls through to the files.
.TP
.I ~/.mu-tickets
GNU Mailutils' own credentials file, one
.I scheme://user:pass@host
line per ticket
(scheme
.B *
matches any), URL\-encoded.
Mode 0600.
Checked before
.IR ~/.netrc .
.TP
.I ~/.netrc
The IMAP/SMTP password, keyed by host.
Mode 0600.
.TP
.I /etc/mailrc ", " ~/.mailrc
Read for
.BR "set folder" ,
.BR "set DEAD" ,
and
.BR alias / group .
.TP
.I /usr/sbin/sendmail
The delivery agent used by
.B send
in local mode.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TVMAIL_CONF
Path to
.I tvmail.conf
(default
.IR ~/.config/tvmail/tvmail.conf ).
.TP
.B TVMAIL_MODE
Force
.B local
or
.B remote
for one run.
.TP
.B TVMAIL_MU_CONF
Path to the GNU Mailutils config (default
.IR ~/.mail ).
.TP
.B TVMAIL_MU_TICKETS
Path to the GNU Mailutils tickets file (default
.IR ~/.mu-tickets ).
.TP
.B TVMAIL_IMAP_PASS ", " TVMAIL_SMTP_PASS
Passwords, in place of
.I ~/.mu-tickets
or
.IR ~/.netrc .
.TP
.B MAIL ", " DEAD ", " MAILRC ", " SENDMAIL ", " XDG_DATA_HOME
Override the corresponding local\-mode defaults.
The spool falls back from
.I /var/mail
to
.IR /var/spool/mail ;
.B send
looks for
.BR sendmail (8)
then
.BR msmtp (1)
if
.B SENDMAIL
is unset.
.SH FLOYD
.B floyd
[\fB\-\-tips\fR | \fB\-\-status\fR] [\fITEXT\fR...] says TEXT to Floyd, the assistant behind File > Floyd..., and prints
his answer.  He drives the other verbs (counts, list, purge, empty-trash, vacation), so he can do what the
menus can: "unread", "triage" (unread mail that looks like it is from people), "find mail from NAME about
WORDS [last week|N days] [in archive|trash|sent]", "counts", "archive [older than N days]" (read inbox
messages to the archive; never unread), "empty trash", "unset ooo" / "ooo", "calc EXPR", "convert 5 km to mi",
"time in CITY", "todo [add X|done N]", "note X", "tips" and "level [NAME]".  Archiving and emptying the trash
print a count and wait for "yes" (kept by the persistent
.B serve
process, for five minutes); anything else cancels.
.B \-\-tips
prints the "Floyd not clippy but ..." lines about the mailbox, only at the clippy level, each at most once in
half an hour;
.B \-\-status
prints the level and what it means.  The level comes from $TVMAIL_FLOYD_LEVEL, then
~/.local/share/tvmail/floyd/state.json (what "level NAME" saved), then \fB[floyd] level\fR in tvmail.conf:
off, minimal, helpful, chatty (the default) or clippy.  The tools and levels are tvfloyd.py, found beside the
backend, in ../../tvcommon/py or in ../share/tvmail; without it he has levels and the mail verbs only.
